    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=096636": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Above the Bar) In Mathematics, School of Mathematical Sciences, College of Science, Engineering and Food Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Lecturer (Above the Bar) In Mathematics, School of Mathematical Sciences, College of Science, Engineering and Food Science Permanent Whole Time Post UCC wishes to appoint an academic to the role of Lecturer (Above the Bar) in Mathematics. Applicants from all areas of Mathematics are invited to apply. Reporting to the Head of School, the appointee will play a pivotal role in the development of Mathematics in the School and across the University. The successful candidate will help develop the research profile of Mathematics, working collaboratively with colleagues. These are key academic roles with responsibility for leading or contributing to the School’s delivery of BSc and MSc programmes, driving growth in PhD enrolment, and embedding mathematical research across the Research Clusters within University College Cork’s Futures Research Initiative. The School has an established international partner and the successful candidate will be expected to foster this relationship through practical commitment to internationalization in teaching and research activities. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Martin Kilian, Head of School via email to M.Kilian@ucc.ie or to Professor Robert Osburn, Head of the Mathematics Discipline, via email to robert.osburn@ucc.ie For further information on the school can be found on our website, Mathematical Sciences | University College Cork . University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Lecturer (Above the Bar) Salary Scale: €80,776 - €104,610 (Scale B) / €76,852 - €99,378 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 20th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Dr Martin Kilian Contact Email : M.Kilian@ucc.ie Job ID : 096636 Contact Number : Close Date : 20-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099373": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Above the Bar) In Public Health Nursing, School of Nursing and Midwifery, College of Medicine and Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Lecturer (Above the Bar) In Public Health Nursing, School of Nursing and Midwifery, College of Medicine and Health Permanent- Whole Time Post UCC wishes to appoint to the role of Lecturer (Above the Bar) in Public Health Nursing. The appointee will be expected to contribute to teaching, assessment, scholarly activity and programme development and administration at undergraduate and postgraduate level, as well as engage in relevant research. The successful appointee will hold a professional nursing qualification, have held or hold a registration (or be eligible to register) in Public Health Nursing with the Nursing and Midwifery Board of Ireland. Applicants will be expected to have an academic qualification to Master’s level and be registered for or have completed a PhD / Doctorate. Applicants are also expected to have considerable public health nursing/ community health nursing experience. The School of Nursing and Midwifery was established in 1994 and since then a wide range of programmes have been developed at both undergraduate, postgraduate and doctorate levels. In addition, the School has a growing suite of Continuing Professional Development modules supporting life-long learning. Since 2012, the School has actively engaged in international recruitment and now has a growing body of international students. The School now has over 1,400 students and is one of the largest in the University. The School is committed to excellence and leadership in teaching, practice and research involving close collaborative relationships with health service providers, and national and international educators and researchers. The School holds the Athena Swan Bronze Award and is in the top 30 in QS subject Rankings for Nursing. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Professor Patricia Leahy-Warren, Tel: 021-4901462/1553, Email: headofsonm@ucc.ie For further information on the school please see Nursing and Midwifery | University College Cork University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on Lecturer (Above Bar) Salary Scale: €80,776 - €104,610 (Scale B) / €76,852 - €99,378 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 27th October 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Professor Patricia Leahy-Warren Contact Email : headofsonm@ucc.ie Job ID : 099373 Contact Number : 021-4901462 Close Date : 27-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098377": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Strategy and International Business, Cork University Business School",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Strategy and International Business, Cork University Business School Permanent Whole-Time Post Cork University Business School (CUBS), University College Cork (UCC) wishes to appoint an academic to the role of Lecturer (Below the Bar) in Strategy and International Business. Reporting to the Head of Department , we are inviting applications from those with a background in Strategy & International Business, broadly defined, who are committed to a programme of intellectually rigorous and relevant scholarship that is publishable in top journals and creating wider impact, while also being interested and capable of delivering high-quality, engaged teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Applicants should demonstrate a track record of relevant research and teaching experience. You should identify your research outputs to date and demonstrate your ongoing potential to publish in leading international journals (3 and 4 star ranked journals by the Chartered Association of Business Schools are preferred), and how you may fit the Centre for the Future of Work and the Economy and/or the various UCC Futures. CUBS holds Triple Accreditation from AACSB, AMBA and EQUIS and since 2024 has held an Athena Swan Bronze Award in recognition of its commitment to advancing gender equality. UCC is ranked in the top 250 universities in the world in the QS Rankings and is proud to be ranked in the world’s top 10 for sustainability. Cork University Business School seeks to shape leaders for a sustainable future with implementation of our strategic vision - Making a difference: Vision to Impact 2026-30 -driving what we do. We are a school that believes that the pursuit of profitability and productivity must be better balanced with the health and wellness of the individual, and the wider environmental and social impact of business decisions. We are committed to providing a dynamic, supportive and inclusive learning, teaching, research and working environment, which recognises the diverse backgrounds and interests of both students and staff. If you want to contribute to delivering our mission and advancing our strategic objectives then this post may be for you. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Professor Lawrence Dooley Email: l.dooley@ucc.ie or Professor Mary McCarthy Email: m.mccarthy@ucc.ie. For further information on the Department https://www.cubsucc.com/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Lecturer Below the Bar Salary Scale: €43,028 - €73,909 (Scale B) / €45,116 - €70,327 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Please note that the anticipated start date for this post is August 2027. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 10th November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Professor Lawrence Dooley Contact Email : l.dooley@ucc.ie Job ID : 098377 Contact Number : Close Date : 10-Nov-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099213": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher - Department of Economics, Cork University Business School",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Post-Doctoral Researcher - Department of Economics, Cork University Business School 28 Months Fixed Term, Whole-time Post An exciting opportunity has arisen to join the Department of Economics at Cork University Business School to contribute to a research programme evaluating the implementation of pharmacogenomics and multiomics in clinical practice. The role will focus on conducting a comprehensive Health Technology Assessment (HTA), synthesising evidence on clinical effectiveness and safety, pharmacoeconomic and cost-effectiveness outcomes, budget impact, organisational and social considerations, and ethical and legal issues. The findings will help inform clinical, policy and regulatory decision-making around the implementation of these technologies. A key component of the work will involve developing an ex-ante economic model to identify cost-effective strategies for implementing pharmacogenomic/multiomic markers in clinical practice. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to interdisciplinary research at the intersection of health economics, pharmacogenomics, multiomics, health technology assessment and healthcare policy, with the potential to inform real-world clinical and regulatory implementation. The research group comprises of both health economists and clinicians so it provides an excellent, inclusive environment to conduct health economic analyses. The team have a strong track record of high-impact publishing and have been funded with public and industry grants for health economics research in oncology. The role is funded by Research Ireland and is based in the Department of Economics at Cork University Business School, under the supervision of Dr Ann Kirby. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project **: Identification of a pharmacogenomic signature for anti-B cell precision therapy in membranous nephropathy (CONFUCIUS)** For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Ann Kirby, Senior Lecturer, Department of Economics, Cork University Business School, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4903679; Email: a.kirby@ucc.ie. For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/business-school/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 16th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Ann Kirby Contact Email : a.kirby@ucc.ie Job ID : 099213 Contact Number : Close Date : 16-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099473": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher / Senior Post-Doctoral Researcher - School of Biological, Earth, and Environmental Sciences",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Post-Doctoral Researcher / Senior Post-Doctoral Researcher - School of Biological, Earth, and Environmental Sciences 30 Months Fixed Term, Whole-time Post The postdoctoral researcher / senior postdoctoral researcher will modify an existing GIS-based multicriteria decision support tool in order to designate optimal areas for offshore windfarm development from Clew Bay to the north coast of the island of Ireland to the northern Irish Sea. Building on a model for NW Ireland, the researcher will collate all available spatial datasets of relevance to offshore windfarm development, standardise within a GIS, and consolidate into combined datasets through expert weighted or numerical modelling. Further weightings will be undertaken through a stakeholder-driven Analytic Hierarchical Process or similar. New MOSAIC-generated megafaunal distribution data will be showcased in the mode with its impact qualified. Liaison with government and stakeholder agencies in the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland (UK) will ensure policy relevant outputs. The candidate should be proficient in GIS and Python with, ideally, a familiarity with the offshore windfarm sector and marine geomatics. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project : GSI-based multicriteria decision support tool development for north of Ireland offshore windfarm site suitability modelling For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Professor Andy Wheeler, Professor of Geology and Principle Investigator, School of Biological, Earth & Environmental Sciences, Tel: + 353 (0) 87 6621038; Email: a.wheeler@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/bees/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: Post-Doctoral Researcher: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a (IUA PD1 Salary Scale), Senior Post-Doctoral Researcher: €55,339 - 60250 p.a. (IUA PD2 Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 23rd October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Prof. Andrew Wheeler Contact Email : a.wheeler@ucc.ie Job ID : 099473 Contact Number : +353 87 662 1038 Close Date : 23-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099673": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher - Office of the Deputy President and Registrar",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher - Office of the Deputy President and Registrar 5 Months Fixed-Term, Whole-time Post The postdoctoral researcher will work within the Universal Design Unit. The unit is currently being established to embed Universal Design across all domains of university life. The unit will work collaboratively with internal and external stakeholders to build on existing initiatives in universal design and develop and implement a whole-of-university approach to Universal Design. The post-doctoral researcher will support the principal investigator to review and adapt approaches to researching Universal Design in Higher Education. With the principal investigator, the post holder will design the strategy for collecting and analysing baseline data, identification of key indicators and methodology for monitoring and evaluation. Key duties and responsibilities will include policy analysis and development, research design, stakeholder engagement, support of project management including research ethics, data management and project reporting, and dissemination to diverse stakeholders Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project: Access Universal Design Project For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Prof. Eleanor Bantry White, Dean of Interdisciplinarity and Inclusion, Office of the Deputy President and Registrar, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 4903000; Email: e.bantrywhite@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/registrar/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, 