    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099693": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - School of Applied Psychology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/10/2026 Research Assistant - School of Applied Psychology 10 Months Fixed Term, Part-time Post (0.5 FTE) The FirstDrops project is an interdisciplinary, design-led research programme focused on improving antenatal colostrum collection and hand expression practices for expectant parents. It combines participatory design, motion capture technologies, and clinical expertise to develop personalised teaching tools, redesigned collection devices, and improved storage systems. The project aims to enhance confidence, reduce stress, and improve early breastfeeding outcomes for parents and infants. The successful postdoctoral researcher will be based in the School of Applied Psychology and will lead qualitative, participatory, and stakeholder-facing aspects of the project. They will design and run co-design workshops, contribute to analysis and evaluation, and work closely with technical and clinical partners to translate user insights into impactful solutions. Project : The First Drops Project: Reimagining Perinatal Colostrum Collection & Hand Expression Through CoDesign For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/ The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Kellie Morrissey, Lecturer, School of Applied Psychology; Email: kellie.morrissey@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see: https://www.ucc.ie/en/apsych/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: 50% of €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 9th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Kellie Morrissey Contact Email : kellie.morrissey@ucc.ie Job ID : 099693 Contact Number : Close Date : 09-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099493": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - School of Food and Nutritional Sciences",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Research Assistant - School of Food and Nutritional Sciences 12 Months Specific Purpose Whole-Time Post The Sustainable Food System Research Group (SFRG) led by Dr Aylin Sahin is a team of enthusiastic and ambitious researchers working on solutions to produce healthy food products without harming the environment. The selected candidate will work on an EU-funded project (Sustain-a-Bite) which investigates the potential of fermentation technology for upcycling fruit and vegetable side streams. The project focuses on minimal processing techniques to produce plant-based foods with improved physicochemical and nutritional properties which are highly consumer-accepted. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. Project : Fermentation of fruit & vegetable side streams For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal inquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Aylin Sahin, Senior Lecturer in Sustainable Food Systems, School of Food and Nutritional Sciences, Tel: + 353 (0) 21 490 2880; Email: aylin.sahin@ucc.ie For further information on the hiring unit, please see https://www.ucc.ie/en/fns/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €34,129 - €44,311 p.a (IUA RA Salary Scale) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 16th October 2026. No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Dr. Aylin Sahin Contact Email : aylin.sahin@ucc.ie Job ID : 099493 Contact Number : + 353 (0) 21 490 2880 Close Date : 16-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=099113": {
      "title": "Research Support Officer - Admin Centre for Gerontology and Rehabilitation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/10/2026 Research Support Officer - Admin Centre for Gerontology and Rehabilitation 18 Months Fixed Term Part-Time Post (0.5 FTE) Centre for Gerontology and Rehabilitation is currently recruiting a Research Support Officer. CGR leads on, or is partner on, several large national and international research projects. One key project is the AI4HOPE project. Funded by Horizon Europe, this is a multi-country study addressing palliative care needs for people with dementia. The study aims to improve the detection of distressing symptoms, and to explore the delivery via technology of non-pharmacological interventions that are known to improve symptoms when provided face-to-face or in groups. The successful applicant will work within in Centre for Gerontology and Rehabilitation, under the direction of the Head of Centre. We are seeking an enthusiastic, motivated person with prior experience in providing research support to multi-partner projects, and ideally in health research. The candidate will require excellent research administration, financial, and academic writing skills. For further information on the Centre for Gerontology and Rehabilitation , see https://www.ucc.ie/en/cgr/ Project: AI4HOPE University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives Salary: €61,471 - €73,392 p.a. (Admin III (Grade 6) Scale B) Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Christopher Walsh, Centre Administrator, CGR; Email: christopherwalsh@ucc.ie Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to hrresearchqueries@ucc.ie quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Friday, 9th of October 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector. Contact Person : Christopher Walsh Contact Email : christopher.walsh@ucc.ie Job ID : 099113 Contact Number : Close Date : 09-Oct-2026 12:00 "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/uccrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=098373": {
      "title": "School Manager, School of History",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 School Manager, School of History COLLEGE OF ARTS, CELTIC STUDIES & SOCIAL SCIENCES Permanent Whole-Time Post UCC wishes to appoint an administrator to the role of School Manager. Reporting to the Head of School, the School Manager will be responsible, with the Head of School, for co-ordinating and supervising the operation of all administrative and support functions in the School of History. In order to do so, they will oversee the management the School’s finances in accordance with the policies and procedures set down by the University and play a key role in implementing the strategic plans of the School of History. Please note that Garda vetting and/or an international police clearance check may form part of the selection process. For an information package including full details of the post, selection criteria and application process see https://ore.ucc.ie/. The University, at its discretion, may undertake to make an additional appointment(s) from this competition following the conclusion of the process. Informal enquiries can be made in confidence to Dr Damian Bracken, email: d.bracken@ucc.ie For further information on the Department https://www.ucc.ie/en/history/ University College Cork is committed to being a fully inclusive global university which actively recruits, supports and retains colleagues from all sectors of society. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) are core values under our UCC Strategic Plan 2023-2028. UCC holds a Silver Athena SWAN award in recognition of our commitment to advancing equality in higher education. We value diversity as well as celebrate, support and thrive on the contributions of all our employees and the communities they represent. We are proud to be an equal opportunities employer and encourage applications from everybody, regardless of age, care-giving status, disability, ethnicity, gender and/or gender identity or expression status, nationality, marital status/civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion/creed, and/or sexual orientation. We are committed to supporting all staff through flexible working schemes, family-friendly policies, training and development, and staff networks. We value the enrichment that comes from a diverse community and seek to promote equality, prevent discrimination and protect the human rights of each individual in line with equality legislation. We encourage applicants to consult our Dignity and Respect Policy and learn more about our EDI related initiatives . Appointment may be made on the Admin III (Grade 6) Salary Scale: €61,471 - €73,392 (Scale B) / €58,529 - €69,839 (Scale A). Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate . Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 3rd November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Salary placement on appointment will be in accordance with public sector pay policy. We encourage you to reach out to us directly should you require assistance or reasonable accommodation during the recruitment process. Please note interviews will be conducted either online via Microsoft Teams or in person in the first instance. Candidates may also be required to attend interviews in person if an interview takes place online. Candidates will be notified as appropriate. Applications must be submitted online via the University College Cork vacancy portal (https://ore.ucc.ie/). Queries relating to the online application process should be referred to recruitment@ucc.ie, quoting the job-title. Candidates should apply, in confidence, before 12 noon (Irish Local Time) on Tuesday, 3rd November 2026 No late applications will be accepted. UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CORK IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITIES EMPLOYER Please note that an appointment to posts advertised will be dependent on University approval, together with the terms of the employment control framework for the higher education sector Contact Person : Dr. Damian Bracken Contact Email : d.bracken@ucc.ie Job ID : 098373 Contact Number : Close Date : 03-Nov-2026 12:00 "
